 'slug' => 'screenwriting-fundamentals', 'title' => 'Screenwriting Fundamentals', 'level' => 'Beginner', 'category' => 'Screenwriting', 'duration' => '3 hours', 'featured' => 1,
 'summary' => 'Turn an idea into a screenplay: logline, structure, character, dialogue and correct script format.',
 'description' => 'Every film starts on the page. This course teaches how to develop an idea, structure a story, write believable characters and dialogue, and format and rewrite a script.',
 'outcomes' => "Write a clear logline\nBuild a story with a beginning, middle and end\nCreate characters with wants and flaws\nWrite visual action and natural dialogue\nFormat a screenplay and revise it",
 'lessons' => [
  ['From idea to logline', 14, "<p>A <strong>logline</strong> is one or two sentences that sum up your story: <em>who</em> wants <em>what</em>, and what stands in the way.</p><p>Formula: <strong>When [event], a [flawed hero] must [goal] before [stakes].</strong></p><ul><li>Choose an idea you care about and can <strong>produce</strong> with the resources you have (few locations, small cast).</li><li>Test the idea: can you say it in one breath? Is there conflict?</li></ul>"],
  ['Structure: beginning, middle and end', 20, "<p>Most stories follow a three-part shape:</p><ul><li><strong>Act 1 — Setup:</strong> introduce the hero, their world and a problem that forces them into action (the <em>inciting incident</em>).</li><li><strong>Act 2 — Confrontation:</strong> the hero pursues the goal; obstacles grow; in the middle things change; a low point comes near the end.</li><li><strong>Act 3 — Resolution:</strong> the final confrontation, then a new normal.</li></ul><p>Every scene should <strong>change something</strong>. If a scene could be removed without changing the story, cut or rewrite it.</p>"],
  ['Character and dialogue', 18, "<ul><li>Give each main character a <strong>want</strong> (what they chase), a <strong>need</strong> (what they really need) and a <strong>flaw</strong>.</li><li>Show character through <strong>choices under pressure</strong>, not description.</li><li>Dialogue should sound like speech: short, interrupted, with <strong>subtext</strong>. People rarely say exactly what they mean.</li><li>Give each character a different way of speaking; cover the names and see if you can still tell who is talking.</li><li>Cut greetings, explanations and any line that only gives information; show it instead.</li></ul>"],
  ['Format and rewriting', 16, "<p>A screenplay uses a standard layout so one page is roughly one minute of screen time.</p><ul><li><strong>Scene heading (slugline):</strong> INT. or EXT., place, time — <em>INT. MARKET OFFICE – DAY</em>.</li><li><strong>Action:</strong> present tense, only what we can see and hear.</li><li><strong>Character name</strong> above their <strong>dialogue</strong>; brief parentheticals only when needed.</li></ul><p><strong>Rewriting:</strong> finish a rough draft first. Then rewrite for story, then characters, then dialogue, then polish. Read it aloud and get feedback from people you trust. Free software such as Trelby or Fountain-based editors can handle the format for you.</p>"],
 ],
