 'slug' => 'cinematography-and-lighting', 'title' => 'Cinematography and Lighting', 'level' => 'Intermediate', 'category' => 'Cinematography & lighting', 'duration' => '4 hours',
 'summary' => 'Shape a scene with light: three-point lighting, hard and soft light, colour temperature, lenses, camera movement and lighting darker skin tones well.',
 'description' => 'Cinematography is painting with light and camera. This course builds on camera basics to teach lighting set-ups, lens choice, camera movement and practical ways to light a scene with limited equipment.',
 'outcomes' => "Set up three-point lighting\nUse hard and soft light and control it with diffusion and flags\nMatch colour temperature and white balance\nChoose lenses and camera movements for the story\nExpose and light a range of skin tones well",
 'lessons' => [
  ['Three-point lighting', 18, "<p>The classic set-up for lighting a person uses three lights:</p><ul><li><strong>Key light:</strong> the main light, usually at about 45° to the side and above the subject.</li><li><strong>Fill light:</strong> a softer light, or a reflector, from the opposite side that lifts the shadows. The balance between key and fill sets the <strong>contrast</strong>.</li><li><strong>Back light (rim or hair light):</strong> from behind, separating the subject from the background.</li></ul><p>You do not need three lamps: a window as the key, a white board as fill and a lamp behind as a back light works well.</p>"],
  ['Hard light, soft light and control', 18, "<ul><li><strong>Hard light</strong> (direct sun, a bare bulb) creates sharp-edged shadows and drama.</li><li><strong>Soft light</strong> (overcast sky, a window with a curtain, a large diffused source) creates gentle shadows and is flattering.</li><li>The <strong>larger and closer</strong> the source is to the subject, the softer the light.</li></ul><h4>Controlling light</h4><ul><li><strong>Diffusion</strong> (white sheet, tracing paper, silk) softens light.</li><li><strong>Flags and black cloth</strong> block light and prevent spill.</li><li><strong>Reflectors</strong> bounce light.</li></ul>"],
  ['Colour temperature and white balance', 14, "<p>Light has a colour, measured in <strong>Kelvin (K)</strong>. Candles and tungsten bulbs are warm (about 3200K and lower); daylight is cooler (about 5600K).</p><ul><li>Set the camera’s white balance to match the main light.</li><li>Do not mix colours of light in one shot unless you do it on purpose (for example warm lamp light against a blue window).</li><li><strong>Gels</strong> (coloured plastic sheets) correct or change a light’s colour.</li></ul>"],
  ['Lenses, movement and lighting darker skin tones', 20, "<ul><li><strong>Wide lenses</strong> exaggerate space and depth; <strong>long lenses</strong> compress distance and blur backgrounds. A standard lens near 50mm (full-frame) is close to natural perspective.</li><li><strong>Movement</strong> should have a reason: pan, tilt, dolly, handheld and push-in each create a different feeling.</li></ul><h4>Lighting darker skin tones</h4><p>Many cameras and lighting styles were developed around lighter skin. To photograph darker skin beautifully, <strong>do not underexpose</strong> to protect the background. Expose for the face, use a soft, slightly larger key light, add a gentle rim light to define edges and avoid large amounts of fill that make skin look grey or flat. Check the waveform or histogram and use a skin-tone reference on monitors.</p>"],
 ],
 'questions' => [
  ['In three-point lighting, the key light is…', ['The main light on the subject', 'A light behind the camera', 'The light in the background only', 'Only used outdoors'], 0, 'It is the main light.'],
