 'slug' => 'sound-for-film', 'title' => 'Sound for Film', 'level' => 'Beginner', 'category' => 'Sound', 'duration' => '3 hours', 'featured' => 1,
 'summary' => 'Record clean dialogue and build a simple sound mix: microphones, placement, levels, room tone and basic mixing.',
 'description' => 'Sound is half of the film. This course explains how to choose and place microphones, set levels, capture useful extras and mix dialogue, music and effects.',
 'outcomes' => "Choose the right microphone for a situation\nPlace microphones and set safe recording levels\nRecord room tone, ambience and wild lines\nTell the difference between dialogue, music and effects tracks\nMix a simple scene with clear dialogue",
 'lessons' => [
  ['Why sound matters and the sound team', 12, "<p>Audiences will forgive a soft picture long before they forgive bad sound. Good sound also creates emotion and a sense of place.</p><ul><li><strong>Production sound mixer:</strong> records sound on set.</li><li><strong>Boom operator:</strong> holds the microphone close to the actors, just out of frame.</li><li><strong>Sound editor and re-recording mixer:</strong> clean and balance sound in post-production.</li></ul><p>On a small crew one person can do several jobs — but someone must <strong>always listen with headphones</strong>.</p>"],
  ['Microphones and placement', 18, "<ul><li><strong>Shotgun microphone</strong> (on a boom pole): directional, picks up sound in front. Place it above or below the actors, as close as possible and pointed at the mouth.</li><li><strong>Lavalier (clip-on):</strong> small microphone on the chest, great for interviews and walking scenes; hide it carefully to avoid rustle.</li><li><strong>Handheld microphone:</strong> for interviews and reporting.</li></ul><p>The closer the microphone, the cleaner the dialogue. A microphone one metre from the speaker sounds much more echoey than at 30 cm. Use <strong>windshields</strong> outdoors.</p>"],
  ['Levels, room tone and wild lines', 18, "<ul><li><strong>Levels:</strong> set the recorder so normal speech peaks around -12 dB, with plenty of headroom. Never let the meter hit 0 dB: <strong>clipping</strong> (distortion) cannot be fixed.</li><li><strong>Room tone:</strong> record 30–60 seconds of silence at every location, with everyone quiet, so the editor can fill gaps.</li><li><strong>Wild lines:</strong> re-record difficult lines without camera if noise spoiled them.</li><li><strong>Ambience:</strong> record the sounds of the place (market, wind, birds) for authenticity.</li><li>Use a <strong>clapperboard</strong> or clap to sync separate sound and picture.</li></ul>"],
  ['Basic mixing', 16, "<p>A film mix has three groups: <strong>dialogue</strong>, <strong>music</strong> and <strong>effects</strong> (including ambience). Dialogue is the priority.</p><ul><li>Clean dialogue first: remove clicks, hum and noise gently.</li><li>Set dialogue so it is consistent between shots.</li><li>Lower music and effects underneath dialogue (called “ducking”).</li><li>Use <strong>fades and cross-fades</strong> so edits do not click.</li><li>Listen on headphones and on small speakers (like a phone) and on larger speakers.</li></ul>"],
 ],
