 'slug' => 'film-editing-essentials', 'title' => 'Film Editing Essentials', 'level' => 'Beginner', 'category' => 'Editing & post-production', 'duration' => '3 hours',
 'summary' => 'Organise footage, make invisible cuts, control pacing, add sound and export correctly.',
 'description' => 'Editing is where the film is truly written. This course teaches an organised workflow, the main types of cuts, pacing and finishing a film for screens and the internet.',
 'outcomes' => "Ingest, organise and back up footage\nMake match cuts, cutaways, J-cuts and L-cuts\nControl pace and rhythm\nCheck continuity and screen direction\nExport a film in a suitable format",
 'lessons' => [
  ['Workflow: ingest, organise, back up', 12, "<ol><li><strong>Copy</strong> all footage to two drives before you begin.</li><li><strong>Organise</strong> in folders: footage, audio, music, graphics, project files, exports.</li><li><strong>Name</strong> clips clearly and use bins (folders) by scene.</li><li>Make an <strong>assembly</strong>: put the best takes of each scene in order, long and rough.</li><li>Then do a <strong>rough cut</strong>, a <strong>fine cut</strong> and a <strong>picture lock</strong> (no more picture changes) before final sound and colour.</li></ol><p>Free editors such as DaVinci Resolve (free version), Shotcut, Kdenlive and OpenShot can do all of this.</p>"],
  ['The cuts that tell the story', 20, "<ul><li><strong>Cut on action:</strong> cut in the middle of a movement so the cut is hidden.</li><li><strong>Match cut:</strong> two shots linked by similar shape, colour or movement.</li><li><strong>Cutaway:</strong> a shot of something else (a detail, a reaction) used to hide a jump or add information.</li><li><strong>J-cut and L-cut:</strong> the sound of the next shot starts before the picture (J), or the sound of the first shot continues over the next picture (L); they make scenes flow.</li><li><strong>Jump cut:</strong> two shots of the same subject from a very similar angle, which feels like a jump in time.</li></ul>"],
  ['Pace, rhythm and continuity', 16, "<ul><li><strong>Pace:</strong> shorter shots speed up the feeling; longer shots slow it down. Vary it to keep attention.</li><li>Cut <strong>when the audience has understood</strong> the shot — not before and not long after.</li><li>Keep <strong>screen direction</strong> consistent so people do not seem to change direction.</li><li>Watch for <strong>continuity errors</strong> (glass half full, then full).</li><li>Cut dull moments, even ones you loved filming. Every shot must earn its place.</li></ul>"],
  ['Sound, titles and export', 14, "<ul><li>Cut dialogue first, then add <strong>music and effects</strong>.</li><li>Check that audio levels are steady and not distorted.</li><li>Keep text on screen <strong>inside the safe area</strong> and large enough for phones.</li><li><strong>Export:</strong> H.264 (MP4) at the same resolution and frame rate as your project is a good choice for online and phone viewing. Keep a high-quality master file as well.</li><li>Watch the full export from start to end before sharing.</li></ul>"],
 ],
