 'slug' => 'producing-and-budgeting', 'title' => 'Producing, Scheduling and Budgeting', 'level' => 'Intermediate', 'category' => 'Producing & production management', 'duration' => '4 hours', 'featured' => 1,
 'summary' => 'Plan and deliver a film on time and on budget: script breakdown, schedules, budgets, permissions, call sheets and safety.',
 'description' => 'Producers make films happen. This course covers the stages of production, how to break down a script, build a schedule and budget, handle permissions and run a safe, organised shoot.',
 'outcomes' => "Describe the producer’s role through each stage of production\nBreak down a script and make a shooting schedule\nBuild a budget with contingency\nSecure location permissions and releases\nWrite a call sheet and plan set safety",
 'lessons' => [
  ['The producer and the stages of production', 14, "<p>Producing means finding the money, assembling the team and managing the film from idea to audience.</p><ul><li><strong>Development:</strong> the idea, script and funding plan.</li><li><strong>Pre-production:</strong> budget, schedule, casting, locations, permits and crew.</li><li><strong>Production:</strong> the shoot.</li><li><strong>Post-production:</strong> editing, sound, colour, music and titles.</li><li><strong>Distribution:</strong> festivals, online release, screenings and marketing.</li></ul>"],
  ['Script breakdown and schedule', 18, "<p>A <strong>script breakdown</strong> lists everything each scene needs: cast, props, costumes, locations, vehicles, special effects, extras and special equipment.</p><ul><li>Group scenes by <strong>location</strong> and by <strong>cast availability</strong> so you do not travel back and forth.</li><li>Estimate time honestly: dialogue scenes and anything with children, animals, crowds or night shooting take longer.</li><li>Build in <strong>weather cover</strong> and rest days.</li><li>Shoot the hardest and most important scenes <strong>early</strong>, not on the last day.</li></ul>"],
  ['Budgeting', 20, "<p>A budget lists all the costs. Typical groups are:</p><ul><li><strong>Above the line:</strong> story rights, writer, producer, director, principal cast.</li><li><strong>Below the line:</strong> crew, equipment, locations, transport, food, art department, insurance.</li><li><strong>Post-production:</strong> editing, sound, colour, music licences, subtitles.</li><li><strong>Distribution and marketing:</strong> festival fees, deliverables, posters, trailers.</li></ul><p>Always add a <strong>contingency</strong> (commonly around 10%) for surprises. Track real spending against the budget every day. Free templates are available from several film sites.</p>"],
  ['Permissions, call sheets and safety', 18, "<ul><li><strong>Location permission:</strong> get written permission from owners and, where required, local authorities.</li><li><strong>Releases:</strong> have cast, crew and anyone identifiable in your film sign a written agreement that allows you to use their image and voice.</li><li><strong>Insurance:</strong> check what is needed for equipment, people and locations.</li><li><strong>Call sheet:</strong> a one-page plan for the next day: call times, scenes, locations with a map, cast and crew contacts, weather, nearest hospital.</li><li><strong>Safety:</strong> do a risk check at each location, have a first-aid kit, never rush dangerous scenes and look after the cast and crew with water, food and rest.</li></ul>"],
 ],
 'questions' => [
  ['What is a script breakdown?', ['A list of everything each scene requires', 'A broken script', 'A review of the film', 'A film poster'], 0, 'It identifies cast, props, locations and more.'],
  ['Why group scenes by location?', ['To save travel and set-up time', 'To confuse the crew', 'To avoid actors', 'To remove dialogue'], 0, 'Fewer location moves save time and money.'],
